// Lesson 7.7 — Level Studio II + Cross-Playtest
// Polish two beats, then the cross-playtest ritual with a beat-granular table.
// The run is entrance-to-GOAL (the lever door / collectible / exit the student
// built in 7.5-7.6). There is no boss door and no boss in this project, and no
// spawn-to-boss run. The unit closes and Unit 8 opens with boss design.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>7.7 — Level Studio II + Cross-Playtest</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Yesterday you built it; today a stranger meets it. Polish the two beats that most need it,
    then run the cross-playtest — a different partner, the full cave from the entrance to your
    goal, and numbers on paper instead of opinions. This closes the level-building arc of the
    semester.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Studio Brief: Two Beats, Deeply</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        From yesterday's walk ritual and your written plan (<a href="7.6.html">7.6</a>), you
        already know which two beats need work. Polish means these four things, and nothing else:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Sightlines:</strong> trim or add tiles until the next beat is glimpsed from the current one. Every removed tile is a question the player stops asking.</li>
        <li><strong>Telegraphing:</strong> the turret is visible at rest before it fires — exactly as you tuned it in <a href="3.4.html">3.4</a>. A threat the player never saw is not difficulty, it is an ambush.</li>
        <li><strong>Reward placement:</strong> coins on the route you <em>want</em> walked. A coin trail is a dotted line the player follows without ever noticing they are being led.</li>
        <li><strong>Death-audit repair:</strong> the beat where dying costs the most gets a shorter approach, or a checkpoint you build yourself — a trigger that stores a position and a respawn that reads it. Your respawn loop from <a href="2.2.html">2.2</a> is all you get unless you build more.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Do not polish everything. Two beats, deeply. That is how studio time works under a
        deadline, and your deadline is the cross-playtest, which starts in twenty minutes.
    </p>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>Polishing moves colliders, and moving colliders breaks things three beats away.</strong>
        Every tile you erase or paint on <code>Collision</code> changes the shape the player
        stands on. A ladder zone you sized against yesterday's floor may now be one tile short.
        A spike you placed against yesterday's ledge may now hover over nothing. Before the
        playtest, walk the whole cave once and look specifically at everything you touched — not
        at the two beats you polished, at everything downstream of them.
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the pre-test audit.</strong> Answer these before
        your tester sits down, in writing, on the same page as the table:
        <ul>
            <li>What is your cave's goal, in one sentence — the lever door, the collectible, or the exit? Say what the player must do to finish.</li>
            <li>How long is the full run, measured with a stopwatch yesterday and re-measured now? Did polishing change it?</li>
            <li>Which beat do you predict will kill your tester most often, and how many deaths do you predict there? Write the number down <em>before</em> the test — a prediction you cannot be wrong about is not a prediction.</li>
            <li>Where does the player first see the goal, or evidence that a goal exists?</li>
            <li>If the tester gets stuck and you are not allowed to speak, what is your rescue plan? There is one, and it is a change you make between attempts, not a hint you give.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>


<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Every field that watches people has had to invent a unit of measurement. Read each of
        these for what it does in isolation, then work out which column of your playtest table it
        is telling you to be careful about.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A hospital's hand-hygiene audit:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Count per station, never per shift. One number for the whole
// ward hides the one sink nobody uses.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A supermarket's checkout timing study:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Time per basket, not per queue. The average is a story;
// the distribution is the finding.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — An airport security throughput trial:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The stopwatch starts when the tray reaches the belt,
// not when the passenger walks up.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — A traffic engineer's junction survey (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Count the near-misses, not only the collisions. No crashes
// is not evidence of a safe junction.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A usability lab's think-aloud protocol (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// Record what the participant DID before what they SAY they
// did. The two disagree constantly.</code></pre>

    <ul>
        <li>Example 1 refuses to average over a whole ward. Your table has one row per beat — what would a single total for the cave hide about your level?</li>
        <li>Example 3 defines exactly when the clock starts. When does your stopwatch start for each beat, and would two testers agree on that moment?</li>
        <li>Example 4 counts near-misses, not only disasters. A beat where nobody dies but everybody hesitates is a near-miss — what on your table would record it?</li>
        <li>Example 5 separates what people did from what they said. Which column of your table holds observations, and which one is in danger of holding your interpretation?</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Cross-Playtest</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        New partner — not yesterday's. Silent watching, per the rules you set in
        <a href="2.4.html">2.4</a>. The full cave, entrance to goal. Record per <strong>beat</strong>,
        not per session, because the beat is the unit of analysis. This table is the exact habit
        that becomes a dataset in <a href="9.5.html">9.5</a>.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Beat</th><th>Deaths</th><th>Time (sec)</th><th>Tester said / did</th><th>Fix candidate</th></tr>
        <tr><td>1 Entrance</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>2</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>3</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>4</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>5</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Goal</td><td></td><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Fill the "tester said / did" column with what you <em>observed</em>, not with what you
        assume they meant. "Stopped for four seconds at the ladder base and looked left" is data.
        "Confused by the ladder" is a story you invented.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Then swap.</strong> You play their cave with the same table. You are both designer
        and tester today — notice how different the two jobs feel, and notice which one you are
        better at.
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: What the Numbers Are For</h3>
        <ol>
<li>Why record deaths per beat rather than one total for the run? What does a total hide that a per-beat row reveals?</li>
<li>Your prediction from earlier is written down. Where did reality disagree with it most, and was that a design error or a measurement error?</li>
<li>A beat with zero deaths could be a perfectly fair beat or a completely skipped one. What else on the table tells the two apart?</li>
</ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Polish two beats deeply: sightline, telegraph, reward route, death-audit repair</li>
        <li>Run a beat-granular playtest and record deaths, time and observed behaviour</li>
        <li>Apply exactly one fix chosen from tester data, and re-verify it</li>
        <li>Compare your predicted difficulty curve against the measured one and say what you got wrong</li>
        <li>State what your cave's goal is, and that a tester reached it</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-07/index.html">Unit 7: Level Building &amp; Ladders</a> — the studio and playtest ritual</li>
        <li><a href="../data/playtests.csv">Playtest data (CSV)</a> — what collected data looks like at scale</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the components behind everything you placed</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Next: <a href="8.1.html">8.1 — Boss Design: Spec It First</a>, where Unit 8 begins and you build the thing your cave deliberately does not have.</p>
</div>`;
